   "source": [
    "## Conclusion\n",
    "\n",
    "**Best model:** Random Forest — test R² = **0.945**, RMSE ≈ 465, MAE ≈ 254 vehicles/hour.\n",
    "Polynomial (degree 2) was basically tied at R² = 0.944, and Gradient Boosting followed at 0.917.\n",
    "\n",
    "**Main takeaway:** in the first version the time\n",
    "fields (`hour`, `day_of_week`, `month`) were scaled as plain numbers, which made\n",
    "the linear models almost useless (R² was around 0.18). One-hot encoding them\n",
    "instead — treating them as categories — pulled Linear / Ridge / Lasso up to about\n",
    "**0.83**, so the whole comparison finally makes sense. The tree based-models were\n",
    "already fine because they can split on the raw hour number, and they still come\n",
    "out on top.\n",
    "\n",
    "**Linear vs tree/non-parametric:** the tree-based models (Random Forest, Gradient\n",
    "Boosting) capture the two rush-hour peaks better than the linear family. ElasticNet\n",
    "lagged (0.61) because its default L1/L2 penalty shrinks the one-hot hour columns too\n",
    "hard.\n",
    "\n",
    "**Tuning:** GridSearch on Ridge and Lasso landed on alpha = 1.0 / 0.1, i.e. almost\n",
    "the same as the baselines, so there was no real gain there — The linear regression-based models achieved R² values around 0.83, while the tree-based and polynomial models achieved higher R² values on the test set.\n",
    "\n",
    "**Cross-validation:** 5-fold CV on the top 2 models was stable — Random Forest\n",
    "averaged R² = 0.945 (std ≈ 0.002), matching the held-out test score, so it is not\n",
    "overfitting.\n",
    "\n",
    "**Feature importance:** the hour-of-day dummies dominate, followed by `is_weekend`\n",
    "and `temp_celsius`. That lines up with the EDA — traffic is mostly about *when* it\n",
    "is, not the weather.\n",
    "\n",
    "**Recommendation:** Random Forest achieved the highest test R² among the evaluated models, while Polynomial Regression (degree 2) achieved a very similar performance. However, Polynomial Regression expands the feature space considerably, so the two models have different computational characteristics."
   ]
